 age (i.e. the month they were born in) of different pupils within the\n year group\n Whether a pupil has been taken into care (e.g. Foster Care) at any\n stage\n The level of deprivation in the area the pupil lives (using the Income\n Deprivation Affecting Children Index)\n\n Damian W. Betebenner Student Growth and Value-added: Redux","The Big Picture\n Contextual Value-Added\n Value-Added Analysis\n Corrupting Influences\n Guiding Wisdom\n\n Value-added analyses can be (n.b., I didn’t say should be)\n conducted on other assessment data including interim data,\n end-of-course assessments, and combinations thereof.\n Some districts in the state are doing this\n\nCampbell’s Law Donald T. Campbell\nThe more any quantitative social indicator is used for social\ndecision-making, the more subject it will be to corruption pres-\nsures and the more apt it will be to distort and corrupt the social\nprocesses it is intended to monitor.\n\n Damian W. Betebenner Student Growth and Value-added: Redux","The Big Picture\n Value-Added Analysis\n Guiding Wisdom\n\nGuiding Wisdom\n\n On Questions John Tukey\n It is better to have an approximate answer to the right question than a precise answer\n to the wrong question.\n\n On Statistical Models George E. P. Box\n All models are wrong but some are useful.\n\n On Understanding Aristotle\n We understand best those things we see emerge from their very beginnings.\n\n On Accountability Sherlock Holmes\n They say that genius is an infinite capacity for taking pains. It’s a very bad definition,\n but it does apply to detective work.\n\n On Theories of Action Confucius\n Tell me and I will forget, show me and I will remember, involve me and I will understand.\n\n Damian W. Betebenner Student Growth and Value-added: Redux"]
